WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.752
Privacy and data protection are operational requirements, not afterthoughts.

00:00:03.752 --> 00:00:07.810
Multi-jurisdiction intelligence work can involve personal data, sensitive business data,

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:11.868
partner information, telemetry, fraud records, incident notes, or regulated records.

00:00:11.868 --> 00:00:17.143
The team has to protect that material while still producing useful decision support.

00:00:17.143 --> 00:00:22.013
Data minimization is the discipline of collecting and sharing only what is

00:00:22.013 --> 00:00:24.042
necessary for the authorized purpose.

00:00:24.042 --> 00:00:28.505
It starts before collection begins and continues through analysis, dissemination, retention,

00:00:28.505 --> 00:00:29.317
and closure.

00:00:29.317 --> 00:00:34.592
If the decision can be supported without names, exact locations, full account identifiers,

00:00:34.592 --> 00:00:39.867
raw messages, or unrestricted logs, those details should be removed, generalized, or protected.

00:00:39.867 --> 00:00:42.302
More data is not always better.

00:00:42.302 --> 00:00:45.954
Unnecessary data can increase compliance burden, create avoidable exposure,

00:00:45.954 --> 00:00:47.577
and make sharing harder.

00:00:47.577 --> 00:00:51.229
Common privacy controls include redaction, aggregation, pseudonymization, access control,

00:00:51.229 --> 00:00:54.476
approved storage, retention limits, and careful product design.

00:00:54.476 --> 00:00:58.128
Redaction removes details that the audience does not need.

00:00:58.128 --> 00:01:00.968
Aggregation shows patterns without exposing individual records.

00:01:00.968 --> 00:01:04.215
Pseudonymization can support analysis while limiting direct identification.

00:01:04.215 --> 00:01:07.461
Access control limits who can view sensitive records.

00:01:07.461 --> 00:01:13.548
Retention rules prevent intelligence workspaces from becoming permanent stores of data that should have expired.

00:01:13.548 --> 00:01:15.171
Privacy also affects dissemination.

00:01:15.171 --> 00:01:18.823
A technical team may need an affected system list.

00:01:18.823 --> 00:01:22.069
A partner may need a sanitized indicator summary.

00:01:22.069 --> 00:01:25.316
An executive may need risk and recommended action.

00:01:25.316 --> 00:01:29.374
A legal reviewer may need source, authority, and handling context.

00:01:29.374 --> 00:01:33.432
These audiences do not all need the same raw material.

00:01:33.432 --> 00:01:36.678
Tailoring the product reduces risk and improves usefulness.

00:01:36.678 --> 00:01:39.113
Data protection includes storage and movement.

00:01:39.113 --> 00:01:43.171
Use approved systems, approved sharing channels, markings, and documented access.

00:01:43.171 --> 00:01:45.605
Avoid informal channels for sensitive information.

00:01:45.605 --> 00:01:49.257
If cross-border sharing, employee data, customer data, government reporting,

00:01:49.257 --> 00:01:53.721
or contractual restrictions are involved, route the issue to qualified reviewers.

00:01:53.721 --> 00:01:59.402
The safest intelligence product answers the decision question with the least sensitive detail necessary,

00:01:59.402 --> 00:02:03.460
clear caveats, and a record of how privacy was considered.
